## Decisions

- **The 5-step pipeline shape is preserved verbatim.** The prior
  cycle's plan landed the 5-step pipeline (brain config → brain
  root directories → playbook entry-point artifact → research
  entry-point artifact → MCP entry); this cycle does NOT touch
  the pipeline's count, order, or step semantics. Only the
  field names destructured from the parser result, the helper
  and step-name vocabulary, and the in-line test fixture body
  grammar change. Re-litigating the pipeline shape would be
  scope creep.

- **Hub-artifact paths stay storage-agnostic by FILE-PATH
  CONTRACT.** Preserved verbatim from the prior plan: the
  playbook entry-point artifact lives at
  `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md` and the research
  entry-point artifact at `research/research.md` regardless of
  storage backend. These are framework contracts; the
  orchestrator does not branch on storage shape, it writes to
  fixed paths. The post-pivot vocabulary calls these "entry-point
  artifacts"; the paths themselves are unchanged.

- **`buildPlaybookHubStep` → `buildPlaybookStep`.** Adjusted from
  the prior cycle. The prior plan's decision noted
  "`buildPlaybookHubStep` keeps its name" because at the time
  "playbook hub" was considered storage-agnostic (the framework
  concept being the structural hub at a known path). The current
  cycle's spec retires `hub` from the storage-agnostic vocabulary
  list entirely — `hub` joins `vault`, `wikilink`, and
  `note`-as-synonym as a token that names a connector-specific
  mental model the framework no longer assumes. The renamed
  helper drops the retired token and the function name remains
  honest about what it builds: an InitStep for the playbook
  entry-point artifact.

- **InitStep `name` field changes from `"Playbook hub"` /
  `"Research hub"` to `"Playbook entry-point"` /
  `"Research entry-point"`.** The InitStep's user-facing name
  surfaces in agent-driven install flows; consistent
  storage-agnostic naming there is part of the spec's "code
  identifiers and inline prose ... storage-agnostic" rule. The
  prior plan's decision called this out for `"Brain vault"` →
  `"Brain root directories"`; the same rule applies to these
  two step names this cycle.

- **`resolveBrainAideBody` is preserved as a sibling helper,
  not refactored.** The prior plan's decision (sibling to
  `resolveBrainAideConfig`, not a refactor) still applies. The
  two helpers do similar work but return different shapes; co-
  locating them above the orchestrator keeps the parallel
  visible. This cycle only touches the field name on
  `resolveBrainAideBody`'s return type.

- **`brainRootExists` heuristic preserved.** The `.obsidian`
  directory check stays as one of the three explicit
  forbidden-token carve-outs. The prior plan's decision is still
  correct; this plan does not touch the helper.

- **Hub steps surface `would-skip` (not throw) on parse failure.**
  Preserved verbatim. The cycle's only change to the step
  builders' parse-failure path is the helper-name rename
  (`buildPlaybookHubStep` → `buildPlaybookStep` etc.) and the
  step-name rename inside the returned InitStep object. The
  `would-skip` semantics, the `instructions` field, and the
  user-remediation flow all stay.

- **`obsidianBrainAideTemplate/` is preserved as the helper
  folder name.** Preserved verbatim. The folder name describes
  bundled CONTENT (Obsidian-flavored prose, Obsidian-flavored
  playbook seed bytes); the storage-agnostic identifier rule
  applies to module code, not to content anchors for the
  bundled template. The parent spec's outcomes.undesired
  carve-out applies.

- **The MCP step's behavior is unchanged.** Preserved verbatim
  from the prior plan. `buildBrainMcpStep`, its four migration
  branches, and its derivation through `parseBrainAide` +
  `interpolateArgs` all stay. Touching them is scope creep.

- **Test fixture body grammar switches to marker pairs in every
  in-line brain.aide string.** The two test groups that build
  in-line custom brain.aide fixtures (the "Existing brain.aide:
  ... source from on-disk file" group and test 5d) currently
  use the retired `## Prose` / `## Playbook hub` / `## Research
  hub` heading grammar. Those fixtures now parse as
  `malformed-body` against the post-pivot parser; their
  assertions about hub-step `would-create` content would never
  fire because `resolveBrainAideBody` returns null for a
  malformed body. The fixtures must switch to the marker-pair
  grammar so the parser succeeds and the tests exercise their
  intended behavior (user customization flows through to the
  entry-point artifact bytes). The retired heading lines are
  REMOVED, not transformed — there are no `## ` headings in
  the new fixtures' body bytes; the marker pairs are the
  section boundaries.

- **The malformed-brain.aide test is INTENTIONALLY constructed
  to trigger `malformed-body`.** That test (in the "Malformed
  brain.aide" group) is the only fixture in this file that
  WANTS to surface `malformed-body`. The current "only
  `## Prose`" shape — which carries the retired heading — now
  fails the marker-pair grammar with a missing-markers reason
  (six markers absent, per the strict-failure migration policy).
  The test's intent is preserved: write a brain.aide whose body
  the parser rejects, assert the hub steps surface
  `would-skip`. The fixture's exact shape can stay
  "only `## Prose`" with no marker pairs, OR can switch to
  "valid prose marker pair, missing playbook + research marker
  pairs" — both produce `malformed-body`. The simpler shape
  (no marker pairs at all) maps directly to the post-pivot
  strict-failure migration policy and is the canonical
  malformed-body case the parser cascade plan tests against.

- **Top-of-file invariant comment block preserved with
  cycle-specific update.** Preserved from the prior cycle. The
  `obsidianMcpEntry` boundary block stays verbatim (still a
  load-bearing rule). The "this cycle" block updates to name
  the new vocabulary: entry-point bytes (not hub bytes) flow
  through the parser from the marker-pair body sections (not
  heading-bounded sections). A reviewer encountering the file
  later understands why the invariant exists and which cycle
  introduced which constraint.

- **Vocabulary sweep is exhaustive on `hub` standalone but
  preserves `study-playbook` (skill name) and `# Coding
  Playbook` (the H1 sentinel inside the playbook section's
  bytes — content the user owns, asserted on by tests).** A
  blanket find-and-replace on `hub` would corrupt those
  carve-outs. The implementor reads each occurrence in context
  and rewrites only standalone `hub` (and compounds like
  `hub artifact`, `playbook hub`, `research hub`) per the
  storage-agnostic vocabulary rule. The MCP tool name
  `mcp__brain__read_note` and similar tokens contain the
  substring `note` — those are tool-name carve-outs and stay.
